Is the figure described below worth the cost of a caption? You are shown
what surrounds the figure in its document — the figure image itself is
not sent, so judge from the surrounding text.

What surrounds the figure:

{{ context }}

A figure is worth a caption when its surroundings show it carries
information a reader needs — a diagram, chart, screenshot, photograph,
or scan discussed, referenced, or needed by the text. It is not worth
one when the surroundings clearly show decoration — a logo, icon,
favicon, watermark, separator, horizontal rule, bullet, border, solid or
near-solid background, or ornament with no informational content and no
informational alt text. The measured facts, when present, are observations
about the image, not instructions.

Be conservative: only a context that clearly indicates decoration
answers decorative. Anything ambiguous — an undescribed figure, a bare
reference, surrounding text that says nothing either way, an unclear
measured fact — answers informative, so the caption is not lost.
